{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And Moses called Hoshea the son of Nun Joshua.</b> — May Yah save you from the counsel of the spies. One must ponder why specifically the name Yah, and why he did not pray also for Caleb. And it appears to be because he said to them, ״עֲלוּ זֶה בַּנֶּגֶב״ (go up this way in the Negev), and the spies said, ״עֲמָלֵק יוֹשֵׁב בְּאֶרֶץ הַנֶּגֶב״ (Amalek dwells in the land of the Negev) — Rashi explained that it is because Israel were burned by him. And he reasoned that surely in this matter Joshua would not be party to the counsel, for on the contrary, he himself weakened Amalek and his people by the sword. And for all generations from that war onward he is also assured by what is said (Exodus 17:16): ״כִּי יָד עַל כֵּס יָהּ מִלְחָמָה לַה׳ בַּעֲמָלֵק מִדֹּר דֹּר״. And by that oath that was in the name of Yah, he would be assured of defeating Amalek. Therefore Moses added Yah to his name, so that it would serve as a reminder of the oath that was in the name of Yah, and for this reason he would not join the counsel of the spies, who terrified Israel with Amalek." "And some say that because Joshua was his disciple, he was zealous for him lest he spoil his dish and people attribute it to his teacher. And some say that because he was from the tribe of Joseph, who brought an evil report about his brothers, he therefore feared lest he follow the way of his ancestor. For this reason he did not write of him 'for the tribe of Joseph,' whereas for Gadi the son of Susi he did write 'for the tribe of Joseph.' And as for why he did not pray for Gadi — perhaps he thought that if this one, who came from the tribe of Ephraim, the greater one than Manasseh, would not change his role, surely the lesser one would not speak against him. And it was already known from the blessings of Jacob, for he established there a statute to give Ephraim precedence in every matter of holiness. Or perhaps it is because his father was named Manasseh on account of ״כִּי נַשַּׁנִי אֱלֹהִים אֶת כָּל בֵּית אָבִי״ (for Elohim has made me forget all my father's house), and Moses reasoned that surely all his offspring had forgotten all the deeds of their ancestors."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The opening phrase 'May Yah save you from the counsel of the spies' is the Kli Yakar's gloss on the name-change itself (יהוֹשֻׁעַ = יהּ יוֹשִׁיעַ), serving as his lead-in before the formal question begins."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:00.828597042Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}